\documentclass{PIScriptDoc}
\script{SubframeQualityReview}
\keywords{WBPP, subframe quality, review, blink, FWHM, eccentricity, PSF signal weight, sky background, autofocus, PHD2, guiding, original lights}
\brief{Review WBPP subframes, compare their quality, and move manually rejected original lights. User guide for version 0.42.2.}

\description{
Subframe Quality Review helps you decide which original light frames to exclude before rerunning WeightedBatchPreprocessing (WBPP). It measures the calibrated and registered images from a completed WBPP run, identifies potentially poor subframes within each comparable group, and lets you inspect them against the best scoring subframe or the linear filter master.

The default shortlist contains the worst 10\% of measured subframes in each group. REVIEW is a suggestion to inspect an image. Only your explicit rejection decisions are included in a move operation. The corresponding original, uncalibrated files are moved to your chosen folder after you confirm the move plan.

The script supports monochrome images and registered, debayered OSC images from one telescope setup. Optional corner measurements help reveal focus or star-shape problems near the edges. Quality plots can show the acquisition sequence and autofocus start times from the Autorun logs in a selected folder.
}

\section{Start and complete a review}{
With TGScripts installed through the standard PixInsight script installation, choose Script > TG Scripts > Subframe Quality Review. The script requires PixInsight 1.9.5 or later. Use the documentation button at the lower right to reopen this guide.

\list[ordered,spaced]{
   {Complete a WBPP run. Keep its calibrated and registered lights and its linear, per-filter integrations. Use masters with the same image dimensions as the registered lights; do not crop or drizzle the masters for this review.}
   {Enter the telescope aperture for FWHM grading, then select the WBPP output directory, the original-light root, and a destination for rejected originals. Optionally select a log folder containing the acquisition and guiding logs.}
   {Click Scan files. Check the Filter / group selector and the Original association column. Each original that can be moved must have a unique, verified association.}
   {Set Review worst (\%) and the quality weights as needed.}
   {Click the magnifier button at the bottom for a field measurement. To include the center and four corners, click the adjacent Measure corners grid button. It reuses existing field results; if these are missing, it measures the field first. Both actions cover all discovered groups and their matched masters.}
   {Review the measurements and quality plots. Select a subframe and open the Blink frame / reference tab, or double-click its row. The image preview loads when needed. Repeating a measurement reuses unchanged results and an unchanged loaded preview. Inspect the images before deciding which originals to reject.}
   {Use the Reject column, the Space key on a selected row, or Reject original in the blink tab to mark unwanted originals. REVIEW flags alone do not mark a file for moving.}
   {Click the green checkmark to review the move plan. Confirm it to move the marked original lights to the rejection destination.}
   {Rerun WBPP with the remaining originals. Refresh its light-frame input list so it no longer includes the moved files. Keep the rejection destination out of the new input selection.}
}

Corner measurements automatically adapt parallel processing to the available CPU and memory. No additional settings are required.

Measurements and pending rejection decisions remain in memory while the dialog is open. Closing the dialog discards them. The New Instance button saves folder and evaluation settings in a process icon; it does not save a measured review session. Loading saved settings starts with no corner measurements.
}

\section{Input files and observing conditions}{
Click the Input files and observing conditions bar to collapse or expand the telescope, folder, and log controls. It opens expanded by default. The window keeps its size, and the active tab uses the space released by a collapsed section. Collapsing it retains the selected paths, settings, measurements, and rejection choices. Saved process instances remember its expanded or collapsed state.

Telescope aperture is at the bottom of this section, aligned with the folder controls. Enter the imaging telescope's aperture diameter in whole millimeters. It is required for FWHM grading and is initially unset; it is not needed to measure or review the images.

Zenith seeing is beside Aperture. Enter an optional seeing estimate in arcseconds at the zenith and a reference wavelength of 550 nm. A positive value enables grading relative to the expected FWHM for those conditions. Leave it at zero to use the fixed Good and Medium seeing limits. A forecast is an estimate, not a measurement of the actual conditions; check its reference conditions before entering it. If it already includes the target altitude correction, convert it to zenith seeing first.

Session SQM and SQM time (local) are beside Aperture and Zenith seeing. Enter the reading in mag/arcsec². Loading the log folder presets the date and local time from the earliest light-frame sequence in its Autorun logs. Flat frames, focusing and plate-solving exposures, and Chinese translations are ignored. Adjust the preset to the actual SQM reading time. A time you enter manually is retained when the logs are reloaded or changed; an untouched automatic preset follows the selected log folder. This distinction is retained in saved settings. If the new folder has no recognized light-frame start, an automatic preset is cleared.

Click Choose beside SQM time to select the year, month, day, hour, minute and second, then Apply. Cancel retains the existing time. If the time is empty, the selector starts at the logged imaging start, then falls back to the selected exposure midpoint when its local time is known, or the computer clock if neither is available. You can also type or paste YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM or YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS directly. The date avoids ambiguity when a session crosses midnight. Log presets keep their local wall time; the existing inferred or custom UTC offset converts the reading to image UTC timestamps. The reading automatically determines the approximate Bortle class.

Zero means that no SQM reading is supplied. In that case, select Sky brightness on the Quality indicators tab to choose a Bortle class from the compact selector to the left of Calibrate sky. Choose a class from 1 to 9, or — when unspecified. The starting selection is class 6. Your manual selection is retained while SQM determines the class automatically.

\table[header]{
   {{Input} {What to select}}
   {{WBPP output} {The completed output directory containing the registered subfolder and, when available, the master subfolder.}}
   {{Original-light root} {A directory containing the original, uncalibrated light files. Subdirectories are scanned recursively. Flats, darks, bias frames, and master files are excluded from original-light matching. The original root must be outside the WBPP output directory.}}
   {{Log folder} {An optional folder containing Autorun_log and PHD2_GuideLog files for the acquisition sessions. Files marked _CHN are ignored.}}
   {{Rejected originals} {The destination for files you manually reject. Relative subdirectories from the original-light root are preserved. This folder is excluded from original-light scanning.}}
}

XISF and FITS light images are supported. Groups follow the WBPP registered-image directories and compatible exposure times, dimensions, and channel counts. Different filters or exposure groups are evaluated independently. Scores and ranks describe relative quality within a group; they are not absolute quality ratings across filters or sessions.

The master is selected automatically from the master subfolder. Its masterLight filename must identify the group's filter. When several masters share that filter, the script looks for the corresponding WBPP group name. A missing or ambiguous match is reported instead of choosing an arbitrary master. The best scoring subframe remains available as a reference when no master is matched.

The bottom message area shows one shared pixel scale in arcseconds per pixel and the camera name, independent of the selected filter. The scale comes from the master metadata's focal length and effective pixel size; X and Y values appear separately when they differ. Hover over the scale to see the master filenames and calculation inputs. Scan again after changing a master file.

The registered images, their associated originals, and matched masters must identify the same camera. A scan stops if different cameras are found. Missing camera names are reported as not fully verified. The same camera can still have a different pixel scale with different focal lengths or binning. Conflicting master scales or different master and registered-image dimensions are reported instead of assuming one shared scale. If no usable master scale metadata is available, the scale is shown as unavailable.

Original matching uses the source filename and acquisition metadata, including observation time, camera, and exposure. An ambiguous match, missing required metadata, or an original associated with more than one processed image prevents that original from being moved. The Original association column and row details explain the result. Master rows are for comparison and can never be rejected.

Changing the WBPP output or original-light root invalidates the current review; scan and measure the new selection.

Scan files is highlighted in slate gray, retaining the standard pushbutton appearance, when a scan is needed: on startup, after changing either image folder, or after an unsuccessful scan. The highlight clears after a successful scan. The magnifier, Measure corners and Undo moved originals buttons are enabled only after a successful scan. Changing measurement or display settings does not require another file scan.

The green Move checkmark and blue Undo arrow also require a Rejected originals folder. Both remain disabled while this field is blank. Their tooltips explain what is required. Entering or clearing the folder updates the buttons immediately; no rescan is needed. Move additionally requires at least one manually rejected original. Scanning and measuring remain available with the rejection folder blank.
}

\section{Quality evaluation controls}{
Click the Quality evaluation bar to collapse or expand the section. It opens expanded by default. The window keeps its size when either section is toggled. Collapsing it retains all settings, measurements, scores, and rejection choices. Saved process instances remember the expanded or collapsed state.

The first column separates Measurements and review from Score selection. Measurements and review has Review worst and Minimum stars in the left column, with Edge guard and Corner size in the right column. Score selection contains the three checkboxes that determine the active Score. Its bold Current Score label identifies the selected R1, R2, R3, or R4 method. Score weights sets the manual weights in two columns. Guiding contains its scoring mode, weight, and thresholds. The active scoring mode is also reported in the bottom message area.

With the R3 and R4 options off, manual scoring uses R1 for field-only evaluation or R2 when corner measurements are included. The strongly correlated KPI option selects R3; the rank-of-ranks option selects R4. These two options are alternatives. The corner checkbox determines whether available corner measurements participate in the selected method.

\table[header]{
   {{Control} {Default} {Meaning}}
   {{Use R3: strongly correlated KPIs} {Off} {Use the R3 correlation score as the active Score. R3 is also calculated and displayed while this option is off. Selecting it turns off Use R4: rank of ranks.}}
   {{Use R4: rank of ranks} {Off} {Use the equal-weight average of normalized R1, R2, and R3 ranks as Score. Omit unavailable ranks and omit R2 while corner scoring is off. Selecting it turns off Use R3: strongly correlated KPIs.}}
   {{Review worst (\%)} {10} {Shortlist this percentage of valid field measurements independently in each group, rounded up. Images with insufficient required measurements are also flagged for inspection, so REVIEW may contain more than the percentage quota.}}
   {{Measure corners} {Button beside the magnifier} {Measure the center and four corners across all groups and matched masters. Existing field and regional results are reused. Missing field results are measured first. Regional results appear in the selected-image measurements; the scoring checkbox controls whether to use them.}}
   {{Include corner measurements (R2)} {Off until measured} {Include or exclude stored corner results in the current score without measuring again. Available once regional results exist. The first corner measurement enables it; subsequent measurement runs retain your choice.}}
   {{Corner size (\%)} {22} {Width and height of each corner and center patch, as a percentage of the common measurement field.}}
   {{Edge guard (\%)} {2} {Inset applied inside the common valid field to reduce edge contamination.}}
   {{Minimum stars} {10} {Minimum star and PSF support required for a valid measurement. Sparse fields or small corner patches may fail this requirement.}}
   {{Quality weights} {See the Score section} {Relative influence of each component. A zero weight disables that component. In manual mode, at least one underlying quality indicator must have a positive weight.}}
   {{Sky KPI} {Sky quality, weight 20} {Choose the single sky indicator eligible for scoring: Sky quality, Transparency, or Sky brightness. Sky quality combines transparency and sky background. The other two remain available for inspection.}}
}

The Sky KPI selector in Score weights applies to R1, R2, Below-mean Quality, and the sky candidate considered by R3. R4 uses the resulting ranks. Only the selected sky indicator can participate; R3 does not substitute another sky indicator if your choice fails its correlation threshold. The shared weight controls manual scoring; R3 uses weight 1 when the selected indicator qualifies. A zero manual weight excludes it from R1, R2 and Below-mean Quality, while R3 still tests the selected indicator as it does other candidates. The selection and manual weight are saved with process settings. Changing the selection recalculates scores from existing measurements without remeasuring images.

Changing the review percentage or quality weights recalculates rankings from existing measurements. Changing Minimum stars immediately reassesses the measured star counts and updates validity and scores; stored field and corner measurements are retained, and no images are remeasured. Measure corners runs the missing regional measurements and updates scores and correlation selection. It does not repeat existing full-image PSF measurements while the images and measurement settings are unchanged. An unsuccessful regional result remains marked as unavailable; clicking the button again does not repeatedly retry it. Changing Corner size or Edge guard clears the measurements and resets the corner status. Click Measure corners to measure the previews with the new settings, or use the magnifier for field measurements.

Include corner measurements (R2) switches between field-only and field-plus-corner evaluation. Turning it off excludes corner penalties and individual corner KPIs, uses the whole-image sky background, and recalculates Score, Below-mean Quality, R3, and R4. Turning it on uses the stored regional results again. Neither action remeasures images. R1 and R2 remain available for comparison, and the regional measurements and plots are retained. Missing results are still reported when their scoring contribution is enabled.

If regional measurements are already available, their quality plots remain available when corner scoring is turned off. The plot indicates that those measurements are excluded from the current Score. Corner-specific choices are hidden when no corresponding measurements exist. In the Correlation plot, only measurements from the current group's subframes enable regional choices; master-only measurements do not.
}

\section{What is measured}{
The script calls PixInsight's SubframeSelector process to measure the registered lights and the matching masters. It uses a shared valid field inside the registered images, excluding registration borders as far as the sampled border checks can determine. FWHM is reported in pixels with one decimal place.

Measurements are performed on temporary, floating-point image crops. This allows signal and noise information to be evaluated for the measured region without reusing inherited WBPP noise metadata. Source images are opened for reading; their pixels and metadata are not changed.

Field measurements cover all groups and their matched masters. Optional center and corner measurements follow, with parallel processing adapted to your computer's available memory and PixInsight processing limits. Existing field measurements are reused when adding corners.

After you click the magnifier, the script checks the common usable field, measures missing field results and any enabled missing regions, and updates the scores. For sky calibration, an SQM reading uses the measured frame nearest its reading time. Without SQM, a selected Bortle class uses Gaia photometry on the best default-scoring frame. Gaia sky-coordinate repair, when needed, can make this last step take longer. The console shows progress. Repeating measurement in the same open review reuses unchanged results and sky calibration. Opening the Blink tab loads the image preview when needed.

With corner scoring enabled, each of the four corners is evaluated for FWHM, FWHM relative to the center, and eccentricity. These measurements use the registered image orientation. After rotation or a meridian flip, a displayed corner does not necessarily correspond to the same physical camera corner.

Measurements describe the processed, registered images. Their PSF signal weights need not equal the values originally recorded by WBPP at an earlier processing stage. Very sparse regions, unusual star profiles, or registration artifacts merit visual inspection even when a numerical score is available.
}

\section{Sky brightness}{
With Include corner measurements (R2) enabled, Sky brightness is the minimum of the arithmetic means of the five previews: Center, Top left, Top right, Bottom left, and Bottom right. With the option off, it is the arithmetic mean of the whole registered image, including any registration borders.

Values are reported in image units. For RGB images, the mean uses the green channel. Stars and extended objects remain part of the means; this indicator is an estimate, not a measurement of empty sky alone. Compare images with the same brightness scale and processing. Background subtraction or normalization can change its interpretation.

Lower values are treated as better quality. To use this indicator in scoring, choose Sky brightness in the Sky KPI selector. R1 uses the whole-image mean; R2 uses the preview minimum. Only this selected sky indicator then participates in Below-mean Quality and is eligible for R3 selection. Switching corner scoring updates the displayed value, Score, R3 selection, and R4.

The Review table reports Sky brightness with six decimal places. Hover over the value to see its meaning and the five preview means when available. Choose Sky brightness on the Quality indicators or Correlation plot tab, or in Sort by. Missing background measurements are shown as unavailable and do not count as good quality. All five preview means are required to establish their minimum; star-detection success is not required for a pixel mean.
}

\section{Transparency}{
Transparency measures relative stellar throughput by comparing the light received from the same stars in each subframe of a filter group. Higher values indicate more transmitted stellar light. The measurement subtracts each star's local background and accounts for exposure time. RGB images use the green channel.

The subframe with the strongest comparable stellar signal per second supplies the 100\% reference. A value of 80\% means that matching stars delivered about 80\% of the reference's signal per second. This is a relative comparison, not an absolute atmospheric transmission measurement. Small values above 100\% are possible because of measurement scatter or differences in the available stars.

Use the magnifier or Measure corners button to obtain the measurement. The Review table reports the percentage; its tooltip identifies the reference and the number of matched stars. Transparency is also available in Sort by, on the Quality indicators tab, and on either axis of Correlation plot. The plot information button explains the measurement and identifies the group's reference.

At least ten reliable matching stars and a valid exposure time are required. Saturated stars and unreliable measurements are excluded. Missing measurements appear as unavailable, not zero transparency.

The master is measured against the same subframe reference and appears in Review and in the quality plot. It never determines the reference and remains excluded from correlation plots. The comparison uses the master's recorded frame exposure, not its total integration time. Without a valid frame exposure or a reliable subframe reference, the master value is unavailable.

For the master, this is the effective stellar throughput of the integrated image. Brightness normalization during integration can change it, so it does not represent the average atmospheric transparency of the session. The Review tooltip identifies this distinction.

The reference is independent of Score, the current sort order, rejection flags, and whether corner scoring is enabled. Choose Transparency in the Sky KPI selector to use it in scoring. Higher values are better. Sky brightness and combined Sky quality then remain comparison indicators and do not enter scoring separately.

Clouds can reduce transparency, but changing target altitude, focus, and image brightness normalization can also change the result. Compare calibrated, aligned subframes before brightness normalization; a low value alone does not establish the cause. The measurement is reused when adding corners or changing scoring settings.
}

\section{Sky quality}{
Sky quality combines the existing Transparency and Sky brightness indicators. Higher values favour frames with more transmitted stellar light and a darker background. It is available as an additional column in Review, in Sort by, on the Quality indicators tab, and on either axis of Correlation plot.

The combination is Q = (T / Tref) × sqrt(Bref / B), displayed as a percentage. T is Transparency. B is the current Sky brightness value minus any recorded calibration pedestal, divided by exposure time. No separate noise measurement is required. The transparency reference is also the Sky quality reference, at 100\%; values above 100\% are allowed.

For example, unchanged transparency with twice the sky-background rate gives about 71\% Sky quality. A frame with 80\% of the reference transparency and half its background rate gives about 113\%.

With Include corner measurements (R2) enabled, the background uses the minimum of the five preview means. Otherwise it uses the whole-image mean. Changing this option updates Sky quality immediately from the existing measurements. The reference and calculation details are available in the Review tooltip and the plot information button.

Sky quality is a relative estimate of the transparency/background balance, not an absolute sky-brightness measurement or proof of cloud cover. Its relationship to faint-object signal quality assumes comparable camera settings and pixel scales, unnormalized linear data, and exposures dominated by sky background. Stars and extended objects in the background mean, read noise, image normalization, and changes in focus can limit that interpretation. Strong correlation between its two inputs alone does not establish that they measure the same physical quantity or that this combination is superior.

Both indicators, a valid exposure time, and a positive background after pedestal removal are required. Missing or invalid values appear as unavailable. The master uses the same formula and subframe reference, with its recorded frame exposure. It appears in Review and in the quality plot, while remaining excluded from correlation plots. Its Sky quality describes the integrated image's transparency/background balance and is affected by integration normalization; it does not measure the signal-to-noise improvement from stacking.

Sky quality is the default choice in the Sky KPI selector, with manual weight 20. It combines transparency and sky background; higher values are better. Sky brightness and Transparency supply its calculation but do not also enter scoring as separate indicators. R1 uses the whole-image background basis; R2 uses the preview minimum. It uses cached measurements and adds no image reads or star measurements. Adding corners or changing scoring settings reuses the master's stellar measurements as well as those of the subframes.
}

\section{Score and Below-mean Quality}{
Higher Score means better relative quality. Scores range from 0 to 1 and are displayed with three decimal places. The default Score sort puts the lowest, worst scores first. The score combines penalties for weak signal, few detected stars, large or elongated stars, poor results in the selected sky indicator, optional corner problems, guiding errors when logs are available, and the breadth of below-mean quality across indicators.

\table[header]{
   {{Component} {Poor-quality direction} {Default weight}}
   {{PSF signal weight} {Lower} {40}}
   {{Star count} {Lower} {15}}
   {{Field FWHM} {Higher} {25}}
   {{Field eccentricity} {Higher} {20}}
   {{Corner quality} {Higher penalty; included when enabled} {20}}
   {{Selected Sky KPI} {Lower Sky quality or Transparency; higher Sky brightness} {20}}
   {{Guiding quality} {Larger combined guiding error relative to image pixel scale} {25}}
   {{Below-mean Quality} {Higher fraction of indicators worse than their mean} {20}}
}

For each image-based field indicator, the reference is the median of the group's supported subframe measurements. Its scale is the larger of 1.4826 times the median absolute deviation, 2\% of the absolute median, and a tiny numerical floor. The master is excluded from these reference statistics.

For an indicator where higher is worse, the penalty is (value minus median) divided by the scale. For an indicator where lower is worse, it is (median minus value) divided by the scale. Negative penalties become zero, and penalties above 10 are capped at 10. A good result in one indicator therefore does not cancel a poor result in another.

Corner quality is the worst penalty for star size, corner-to-center size ratio, or elongation across the four corners. Lower is better.

Below-mean Quality uses arithmetic means, not medians. It is the percentage of assessed, enabled underlying indicators on the poor-quality side of their mean. Low PSF signal weight and low star count count as worse; high FWHM, high eccentricity, high Corner quality penalties, and high guiding error count as worse. The selected Sky KPI counts once: lower Sky quality or Transparency is worse, while higher Sky brightness is worse. Equality with the mean is not worse. Score and Below-mean Quality itself are excluded from this comparison.

For example, a subframe worse than the mean in three of four assessed indicators has Below-mean Quality of 75\%. Its score penalty uses the fraction 0.75 directly. With the default weight of 20, it adds 0.75 times 20 to the weighted numerator. Setting the Below-mean Quality weight to zero removes this score contribution while keeping the percentage visible.

Score = 1 − sum(weight × penalty) / (10 × sum of weights). Only available components enter the sums. A Score of 1.000 means no measured penalty; 0.000 means the weighted mean penalty reaches its limit of 10. Score describes relative quality within its group, not a probability or an absolute image-quality rating. Rankings use full precision, so values that display identically can have different ranks.

The arithmetic means used for Below-mean Quality include supported subframes in the group, including manually rejected subframes, and exclude the master. The saved manual weights determine which underlying indicators participate: a zero weight excludes an indicator from this comparison. Corner quality counts as one indicator when corner scoring is enabled and weighted. Selecting R3 or R4 changes the active Score without changing Below-mean Quality, its completeness indicator, or its correlation. Changing the manual KPI selection or the corner/guiding settings can change this composite.

Unavailable indicators are not treated as good measurements. An incomplete Below-mean Quality percentage is marked with an asterisk; its tooltip gives the assessed and enabled counts. A partial Score may still be displayed, but missing required data triggers REVIEW, and R1 and R2 are withheld when their required measurements are unavailable. A partial R3 remains available when the field measurement is valid; inspect its missing-data flags.

R3 is calculated when field measurements are available and at least one KPI qualifies. Enable Use R3: strongly correlated KPIs to use its score as the active Score. It selects indicators automatically for each group when their absolute Pearson correlation with PSF signal weight is at least 0.7, accepting both positive and negative relationships. It requires at least three paired subframes with varying measurements. The master is excluded; manually rejected subframes remain included. Strong correlation describes association, not the cause of poor quality, and can be unstable in small groups.

PSF signal weight is the correlation reference only. It is excluded from R3 selection and contributes no direct weight to R3; self-correlation is not used as selection evidence. Each qualifying indicator has weight 1; excluded indicators have weight 0. Manual weight values are retained, including any zeros, and restored when the option is disabled. Corner quality participates when Include corner measurements (R2) is enabled; guiding also requires logs and a guiding mode other than Ignore. The selected guiding mode still controls missing-data handling when guiding is included.

With Include corner measurements (R2) enabled, the FWHM and eccentricity of each of the four corners are also tested separately against PSF signal weight. Each of these eight indicators is included with weight 1 when its absolute correlation reaches 0.7 with at least three valid paired subframes. The Corner quality composite remains a separate candidate. A corner FWHM can qualify even if eccentricity or another corner is unavailable. Center FWHM contributes through the composite's corner-to-center ratios; it is not a separate scoring candidate.

Each selected corner indicator contributes its own penalty to R3. Larger FWHM and eccentricity are worse, regardless of the correlation's sign. Several qualifying corner indicators therefore give corner performance more combined influence. The Below-mean Quality composite retains its manual indicator selection; qualifying individual corner indicators do not redefine it. Disabling correlation scoring restores the manual weights and the single Corner quality contribution.

R3 assesses the same Below-mean Quality values shown in the Review table and correlation plot, and includes this composite only if its own correlation passes the threshold. Score itself is never a selection candidate. If no KPI qualifies, the script reports No strongly correlated KPIs and leaves the R3 score and rank unavailable. With Use R3 enabled, Score retains the manual evaluation, including its weights, review reasons, and ranking. The weight controls show those retained manual values. R4 omits the unavailable R3 rank. This behavior is determined separately for each filter group.

The qualifying R3 KPIs are listed in the bottom message area on every tab. Hover over that report or R3 for each correlation, sample count, and exclusion reason. Changing this option reuses existing measurements.

Clicking Measure corners afterwards measures missing regions and recalculates the selection for every group. When corner scoring is enabled, Corner quality and the eight individual corner indicators are included only when each meets the strong-correlation threshold. Turning corner scoring off excludes them and recalculates the selection using the field-only indicators. Clearing measurements also removes their scoring contributions until they are measured again.

The master provides comparison measurements but receives no Score or R1–R4 ranks. These cells show an em dash. It is excluded from score baselines, rank populations and the REVIEW quota. Its measured quality indicators remain available in the table, measurement plots and blink preview.
}

\section{R4: Rank of ranks}{
R4 combines three views of image quality into one ranking. Each available ranking has equal influence after normalization. It averages the rank positions, rather than the underlying quality scores.

\table[header]{
   {{Component} {What it evaluates}}
   {{R1} {Field quality using your manual weights, without corner measurements.}}
   {{R2} {Field and corner quality using your manual weights.}}
   {{R3} {Quality using equally weighted indicators that strongly correlate with PSF signal weight.}}
}

For each component, normalized rank = (rank − 1) / (N − 1), where N is the number of images with a valid rank for that component in the group. The worst rank becomes 0 and the best becomes 1. If only one image has a rank, its normalized value is 0.5.

R4 Score = mean of the available normalized R1, R2, and R3 ranks.

The R4 column then ranks these combined scores: 1 is worst, and larger rank numbers are better. The combined Score itself ranges from 0 to 1, with higher values indicating better relative quality. Rank and Score are different values.

For example, suppose each component ranks 11 images and one image has R1 = 3, R2 = 7, and R3 = 9. Its normalized ranks are 0.2, 0.6, and 0.8. Its combined Score is (0.2 + 0.6 + 0.8) / 3 = 0.533. Its R4 position depends on the combined scores of the other images. If only R1 and R3 are available with those values, the combined Score is (0.2 + 0.8) / 2 = 0.500.

R2 is omitted when Include corner measurements (R2) is off or that image has no valid R2. Other unavailable ranks are also omitted; they never count as zero. An image with no available ranks has no R4. A partial result can still require REVIEW. Corner scoring also affects which indicators participate in R3, so switching it can change both R3 and R4.

To use this method, enable Use R4: rank of ranks in Score selection. The Score column, REVIEW shortlist, and best-subframe comparison then use the combined score. Use R3: strongly correlated KPIs selects the R3 score instead; the two options are alternatives. Leaving both off uses manual scoring. R1–R4 remain visible in every mode when their required measurements exist, and changing modes does not measure images again.

Select R4: rank of ranks in Sort by to order the table by R4, independently of the active Score mode. Hover over an R4 value to see the contributing ranks, their population sizes, normalized values, and any omissions. R4 uses all ranked subframes in the group regardless of the table filter, excludes the master from each population, and resolves equal scores by filename.

Manual weights remain editable in rank-of-ranks mode because they determine R1 and R2. R3 uses its independently selected, equally weighted indicators. Below-mean Quality has the same definition in every scoring mode. The Main issue and Score tooltip identify the ranks that most reduce the combined score.
}

\section{Review and measurements}{
Below the table, Reading the table explains R1–R4, the score direction, Below-mean Quality, and the missing-data symbols. Selected image shows a subframe's keep or reject decision, REVIEW status, Score, and main issue. For the master, it identifies its exclusion from scoring and ranking. Its compact measurement table aligns FWHM, eccentricity, and star count across the field, center, and four corners.

Click the Review information bar to collapse or expand both panels together. Collapsing them gives the review table more room without resizing the window or changing the selected image. This preference is saved with process-instance settings.

Hover over the selected-image assessment for the full explanation, or over its measurements for unavailable-region details. The filename is shown separately; hover over it for the full original or moved-file path and the registered-image path. Selecting another image updates these details without changing the window size.

\table[header]{
   {{Column} {Meaning}}
   {{R1} {Rank using the manual weights for field quality and its Below-mean Quality contribution, without corners. Rank 1 is worst.}}
   {{R2} {Rank using the manual weights for field and corner quality, including the corresponding Below-mean Quality contribution. Requires complete regional measurements. Rank 1 is worst.}}
   {{R3} {Rank using equally weighted, strongly correlated indicators other than PSF signal weight. Available when field measurements exist and at least one KPI qualifies, independently of the selected scoring mode. Rank 1 is worst.}}
   {{R4} {Rank of ranks, based on the equal-weight average of the normalized available R1, R2, and R3 ranks. R2 is omitted while corner scoring is off. Rank 1 is worst.}}
   {{Reject} {Your manual rejection choice. Clicking this column toggles the choice for an eligible original.}}
   {{Review} {REVIEW identifies a shortlist candidate or an image requiring inspection because of missing data. Hover over REVIEW to see the reasons for that image.}}
   {{Image} {The measured registered image or integrated master.}}
   {{Acquisition time (UTC)} {The complete date and time used for chronological sorting and quality plots. Master and unavailable timestamps are shown as a dash.}}
   {{Score} {The current quality score, from 0.000 to 1.000; higher is better.}}
   {{PSF weight, FWHM (px), Stars} {Field measurements used in scoring.}}
   {{Sky brightness} {Lowest mean of the center and four corner previews when corner scoring is enabled; otherwise the whole-image mean. Image units; lower is better.}}
   {{Main issue} {The enabled component with the largest weighted impact, such as Weak signal, Few stars, Large stars, Elongated stars, Large corner stars, High sky brightness, Medium guiding, Bad guiding, or Below-mean Quality. Good guiding is reported when no enabled quality penalty applies.}}
   {{Field eccentricity} {Field star elongation, from zero for round stars toward one for elongated stars. Lower is rounder.}}
   {{Corner quality} {The largest corner-quality penalty across the four corners. Lower is better. Stored results remain visible when corner scoring is turned off.}}
   {{Below-mean Quality} {Percentage of assessed indicators with worse-than-mean quality; lower is better.}}
   {{Guiding quality} {Combined guiding error: (minimum + 2 times average + maximum) / 4, in arcseconds. Lower is better. A value requires usable guiding coverage.}}
   {{Guide avg, Guide min, Guide max} {Arithmetic average, minimum, and maximum combined RA/Dec displacement during the exposure, in arcseconds. These are not RMS values. Hover for coverage and guiding quality. An asterisk marks partial statistics excluded from Score.}}
   {{Worst corner} {The corner contributing the largest regional penalty when corner scoring is enabled.}}
   {{Original association} {Whether the corresponding uncalibrated original has been verified for moving.}}
}

All manual Score components have columns: PSF weight, FWHM, Stars, Field eccentricity, Corner quality, Below-mean Quality, Guiding quality, and the selected Sky KPI. Sky brightness, Transparency and Sky quality all remain visible; their tooltips identify which one is selected for scoring. In correlation mode, columns for qualifying individual corner FWHM and eccentricity indicators appear at the right of the table; scroll horizontally to view them. Their tooltips report correlation and sample count. Missing measurements remain marked as unavailable. The quality-column tooltips explain their meaning and current inclusion in Score.

Main issue identifies a scoring contribution, not a diagnosis. For example, elongated stars may be associated with guiding, wind, or optical effects; inspect the image to decide whether to reject it. Hover over the score, issue, or quality columns for contribution details and missing-data information.

R1, R2, R3 and R4 rank subframes only. Their values remain group ranks when the table is filtered; they are not renumbered as visible row positions. Equal scores are resolved consistently by filename. The unscored master stays last when sorting by Score or any rank, in either direction.

Use Sort by in the Sort and filter area. Score, field-width, corner-quality, sky-background, and Below-mean Quality sorts show the poorest values first; PSF signal weight and star count show the smallest values first. R1, R2, R3 and R4 show rank 1 first. The button beside the selector shows Ascending or Descending. Click it to toggle the direction. Choosing another criterion restores that criterion's default direction. The selected criterion and direction are saved in process instances. Below-mean Quality keeps complete comparisons first, with Score resolving percentage ties in the chosen direction; incomplete comparisons follow in both directions.

Acquisition time starts with the earliest full date and time. Toggle to Descending for latest first. In both directions, dated subframes come first, undated subframes follow, and the master is last. Filename sequence numbers do not participate in this ordering. Sorting preserves the selected image and manual rejection choices. The quality plots retain their own order.

Choose Guide minimum, Guide maximum, or Guide average to sort by the corresponding guiding column. These start with the largest error first; use the direction button to put the smallest error first. Values are sorted numerically in arcseconds, including partial measurements marked with an asterisk. Unavailable values follow measured values in both directions, and the master is last. The choice and direction are saved in process instances and also determine the sequence for Rotate subframes (Review order).

Enable REVIEW candidates only to show just flagged subframes. This filter also hides the master. It changes table visibility without marking files for rejection or limiting the quality plots to those rows.

Hover over a REVIEW marker to see why the image was selected. For the Review worst percentage, the tooltip gives its group position, the rounded-up shortlist size, Score, and largest score penalties. It also lists any missing or insufficient measurements and guiding-data issues that require inspection. Both reasons appear when both apply. The explanation follows the current scoring settings and stays consistent when the table is sorted or filtered. REVIEW leaves the rejection decision to you.

When corner measurements are enabled, the REVIEW tooltip also explains unavailable preview measurements individually. It reports the detected-star count and required minimum, a missing or invalid FWHM or eccentricity result, or the recorded processing error. A preview with no result is described as not measured. When the measurement process supplies no detailed cause, the tooltip says so. These details also remain visible for missing regional indicators excluded by correlation scoring; their presence alone does not add a REVIEW flag.

Click a row to select it. Clicking the same selected row again deselects it. Keyboard navigation selects rows; Space toggles rejection of the selected eligible original. Double-click a row outside the Reject column to open it for blinking. Selection, REVIEW status, and rejection are separate states.
}

\section{Blink frame and reference}{
Blink mode defaults to Best scoring subframe. This chooses the highest-scoring eligible measured subframe in the group that has not been rejected or moved. Filter master uses the automatically matched linear integration. The master association is retained for each filter group.

The region selector offers the field, center, and four corners. The preview can display these regions even when optional numerical corner measurements have not been run. Display stretch and brightness alignment affect the preview only. A common reference-derived stretch helps comparison, and brightness alignment reduces global background and contrast differences.

The coordinate strip shows Zoom, X, Y, and Value under the mouse pointer. Value is the displayed preview pixel normalized to 0–1, including any display stretch and brightness alignment, with separate R/G/B values for color images. It has 8-bit display precision. Coordinates are relative to the selected region. The readout follows the displayed image during blinking and updates after zooming, panning, and changing regions; it clears outside the image.

Choose Rotate subframes (Review order) to cycle through the subframes currently visible in Review and measurements. Rotation follows its sorting criterion, direction, and REVIEW candidates only filter. The master is always excluded. It starts from the selected subframe, or the first visible subframe if none is selected, and wraps from the last to the first. The filename and position in the sequence identify the displayed subframe. Keep and Reject apply to that subframe. At least two visible subframes are needed for automatic rotation.

In rotation mode, the first displayed subframe supplies the common preview stretch and brightness alignment. Press Play to start, Stop to pause, or Step to pause and advance one subframe. Previous and Next follow the same sequence and skip the master. Changing tabs pauses rotation.

With Rotate regions set to Off and Filter master or Best scoring subframe selected, press the play symbol to start alternating the selected image and reference. While playing, the button shows a stop symbol; press it to pause. Step pauses automatic blinking and switches to the other image.

Rotate regions offers two ways to inspect all regions of the selected image:

\table[header]{
   {{Choice} {Playback}}
   {{Off} {Keep the selected region while blinking or rotating subframes.}}
   {{Image only} {Cycle through Center, Top left, Top right, Bottom left, and Bottom right, then repeat. Usable field is skipped. No reference image is required; Blink mode is inactive.}}
   {{Blink with reference} {Show the selected image and its reference at the same region, then advance to the next region, including Usable field. Choose Filter master or Best scoring subframe as the reference.}}
}

Region rotation starts at the currently selected region, keeps the same image selected, and preserves zoom. Image only starts at Center when Usable field is selected. Press Play to start or Stop to pause. Step pauses playback and advances one displayed view: the next preview region for Image only, or the reference followed by the next region for Blink with reference. Choosing a preview region manually continues from that region. Previous and Next select another image while keeping the rotation mode. An integrated master can be inspected with Image only, but cannot be rejected.

Region rotation and subframe rotation are alternatives. Enabling region rotation while Rotate subframes is selected changes Blink mode to Best scoring subframe. Choosing Rotate subframes switches region rotation off. Region rotation is off by default, and its choice is saved in process instances.

Interval (s) sets how long each displayed view remains visible, for reference blinking, subframe rotation, and region rotation. Choose 0.10 to 10 seconds; smaller values give faster playback. The default is 0.75 seconds. Regional blinking displays the image for one interval and the reference for another before advancing. You can change the interval while playing without losing your zoom or pan position. In subframe rotation mode, loading the next image adds time depending on image size and storage speed. The interval is saved with New Instance settings.

Previous and Next move through the current visible review list while preserving zoom, pan position, and whether blinking is active. Changing the reference also preserves those states. Switching between Usable field, Center, and the four corners retains the zoom and blink status. The pan position stays within the selected region's bounds. Use Zoom to Fit to fit the current region to the preview.

Switching to another tab and returning to Blink frame / reference retains the zoom and pan position. Selecting another image in Review or through either plot also preserves this view when its preview loads. The position is limited to the new image or region's bounds. The first preview is fitted automatically; use Zoom to Fit whenever you want to refit it. Switching tabs pauses playback; press Play to resume.

Use Reject original or Keep original to change the selected subframe's decision. The master cannot be rejected. Nothing is moved until you use the green checkmark and confirm the move plan.

Reference comparisons require matching image dimensions. A cropped or drizzled master cannot serve as a matching reference for the registered lights; use the corresponding uncropped integration, the best scoring subframe, or Image only region rotation to inspect a subframe without a reference.
}

\section{Quality indicators and acquisition order}{
The Quality indicators tab shows one selected indicator at a time, with Score selected initially. The plots follow WBPP's per-frame style: each image occupies one bar position. They do not aggregate images into frequency bins.

The note beside the selected indicator shows whether higher or lower values are preferable and whether it is included in Score under the current settings. A zero weight, disabled corner scoring, or excluded guiding is identified. In correlation mode, each corner FWHM and eccentricity reports its own inclusion or exclusion. In manual mode these measurements contribute through Corner quality; Center FWHM is used in corner/center ratios. Guide minimum, average, and maximum contribute through combined guiding quality when coverage is usable. Score itself is the combined result. Plotted measurements remain available even when their scoring contribution is disabled.

The blue information symbol button opens the selected histogram’s explanation, including its definition and relevant measurement or calibration details. Reading the plot uses labeled rows for inspecting bars, ordering and the vertical axis. A color-and-marker legend separates image bars, reference lines and quality shading, with a sample and written meaning for each. SQM and autofocus markers appear only when relevant to that plot. These explanations are kept in the information window, without repeating them in plot headings or tooltips. The direction of quality and current Score inclusion remain visible beside the selector. The footer shows only the frame count and order. Hover over individual bars for their image-specific values.

Choose Order by: KPI value for ascending values, or Acquisition time for the acquisition sequence. The plot order is independent of the review table. In chronological plots the master is first, dated subframes run from earliest to latest, and undated subframes follow. Bar spacing is uniform; the horizontal axis is an image sequence, not a proportional elapsed-time axis.

\table[header]{
   {{Color or marker} {Meaning}}
   {{Cyan} {Currently selected image. This highlight takes precedence over the normal bar color.}}
   {{Purple} {REVIEW candidate.}}
   {{Blue} {Other subframe.}}
   {{Red} {Manually rejected or moved original.}}
   {{Gold} {Master, with a master marker.}}
   {{Green dashed line} {Mean of available values for subframes that have not been rejected or moved. The master is excluded.}}
   {{Orange dashed line and AF tag} {Autofocus start within the current group's acquisition-time range, with the final focus position when available.}}
}

Hover over a bar to see its filename, value, status, and acquisition time. Click it to select the corresponding row in Review and measurements. If necessary, the script clears REVIEW candidates only to reveal the selected image, including the master. Clicking a plot bar does not change rejection decisions.

Except for the category plots described below, the vertical range follows the subframe values with approximately 10\% padding. An outlying master value is clipped at the plot boundary and marked with an arrow; its actual value remains visible. This keeps subframe differences readable when the integrated master has a much larger PSF signal weight. Missing values retain their place in the sequence but have no value bar.

Field, center, and corner FWHM plots can show Good, Medium, and Bad background bands using your telescope and reference observing conditions. The FWHM grading settings icon is shown only for Field, Center and corner FWHM plots. Open it to configure the limits. The FWHM grading section below explains the parameters and calculation.

Field, center, and corner eccentricity plots follow the plotted values, including the master, with about 10\% headroom and a small lower margin, bounded by zero and one. Green, amber, and red background bands are clipped to the visible range. Good eccentricity is at or below 0.42; Medium is above 0.42 and at or below 0.60; Bad is above 0.60. The legend and dotted lines mark these boundaries in both KPI-value and acquisition-time order. Hover over a bar to see its category. Eccentricity is dimensionless: zero is round and values approaching one indicate increasingly elongated stars. These ranges are practical inspection guidelines. Score continues to compare eccentricity within the group; the bands do not change scoring or automatically reject an image.

For Guide minimum, Guide average, and Guide maximum, green, amber, and red background bands mark Good, Medium, and Bad guiding. The legend gives the boundaries in arcseconds, and dotted lines separate the bands. Guide minimum and Guide average follow their measured values with a small margin above and below, like Guiding quality. Only the portions of the bands within that range are shaded. Guide maximum includes zero and all three categories, as well as the measured values. These bands appear in both KPI-value and acquisition-time order when the imaging scale is available. The bar colors still identify selection, review, and rejection status. No category boundaries are drawn when the imaging scale is unavailable.

The Guiding quality plot shows the combined indicator without category bands. Its information window gives the definition: G = (minimum + 2 times average + maximum) / 4, in arcseconds. The average contributes 50 percent, and the minimum and maximum each contribute 25 percent. Lower values are better. The displayed minimum, average, and maximum categories help inspect individual guiding measurements; the combined value G determines the guiding penalty used in Score.

The Score and Below-mean Quality histograms show subframes only; the master is omitted.

The green plot mean excludes rejected subframes and can change as you make decisions. The scoring baselines and the arithmetic means behind Below-mean Quality use all supported subframes in the group, including rejects. These intentionally serve different purposes.

Acquisition timestamps come first from the verified original's DATE-OBS or DATEOBS metadata, then from the registered image. Separate TIME-OBS or TIMEOBS fields are supported when the date field contains only a date. Explicit timezone offsets are converted to UTC; timestamps without an offset are interpreted as UTC. The table shows the same UTC timestamp used by the plots.

Acquisition filenames may show a local clock or an end time while the header records a UTC exposure start. The displayed Acquisition time (UTC) is the authoritative value used by this script. The filename counter suffix is ignored.
}

\section{Sky-background comparison}{
With Session SQM supplied, enter the reading's local date and time beside it. The script automatically estimates the Bortle class and selects the eligible subframe whose exposure midpoint is nearest to that time in each directly calibrated group. Masters, rejected or moved frames, and frames without usable exposure times or backgrounds are excluded. Equal time differences prefer the earlier exposure. Click the information symbol button on the Quality indicators tab to open the reference frame, reading time, exposure midpoint in UTC, time difference and reference background. With Gaia calibration, this information window shows the reference, accepted-star count and calibration scatter. It also lists the SQM-to-Bortle ranges used by the script, including exact boundary handling and the shared range for classes 8–9. The table adds the calibrated sky-background range in the plot's image units and the shading color for each class. The shading legend identifies the Bortle classes covered by each color. Before calibration, the SQM ranges remain available; calibrated values and shading show a dash.

The selected frame's measured background is assigned the SQM reading directly. Other backgrounds are converted relative to it. Gaia is not used when an SQM reading is supplied. Missing or ambiguous reading times leave calibration unavailable; enter the complete date/time and check the UTC offset. A missing SQM time never switches to Gaia automatically.

Separate red and blue filter groups also show sky-background shading and the SQM line. They use the green group's calibration, adjusted to their own background levels using the closest-in-time pair of eligible green and red or blue exposures. This uses existing measurements; no additional photometry is needed. The plot caption says relative to green, and the information button shows the calibration reference, paired exposures, time gap and measured backgrounds. The calibrated Bortle table uses the selected filter's image units.

This transfer assumes similar sky conditions between the paired exposures and a stable relative filter response across the group. It is a relative estimate, not independent red/blue sky photometry. Large time gaps, changing sky colour, clouds or transparency can affect it. Red and blue shading requires a compatible measured green group with usable timestamps. Without one, the information button explains why shading is unavailable. With no SQM reading, red and blue reuse the green group's Gaia calibration.

The compact plot caption shows the Bortle class and SQM reading or Gaia calibration. The footer shows the frame count and plot order. The vertical axis follows the plotted backgrounds, including the master when present, with a small margin below and headroom above. Bortle limits do not extend it. Only the parts of the quality bands inside that range are shaded.

The pink dashed line marks the SQM reading on the image-unit axis. Bars below it indicate darker backgrounds; bars above it indicate brighter backgrounds. With corner scoring enabled, calibration uses the same minimum preview mean as the plot; otherwise it uses the whole-image mean. Switching this basis reuses the measured backgrounds.

Without an SQM reading, choose Sky brightness on the Quality indicators tab. The Bortle class selector appears immediately to the left of Calibrate sky. Local Gaia photometry calibrates the unrejected subframe with the best default field-only score in each directly calibrated group. Changing score weights or choosing R2, R3 or R4 does not change that selection rule. Choose — to disable Bortle shading and automatic Gaia calibration.

Calibration runs after image measurements or when the required sky settings are supplied afterwards. Existing field and corner measurements are retained. Calibrate sky is shown only while the current group has no usable calibration; it disappears after success and reappears if calibration becomes unavailable. Changing Bortle class reuses Gaia calibration. Changing the SQM reading or time updates the direct calibration using existing background measurements. Returning to a previously calibrated mode reuses its result when still applicable.

The plot is shaded automatically when calibration and a Bortle class are available:

\table[header]{
   {{Category} {Meaning}}
   {{Good, green} {Darker background corresponding to lower Bortle classes.}}
   {{Medium, amber} {Background within the selected or inferred class's approximate brightness range.}}
   {{Bad, red} {Brighter background corresponding to higher Bortle classes.}}
}

For example, class 6 uses approximately 18.94–19.50 mag/arcsec² for Medium. Class 1 has no lower-class Good band. Classes 8 and 9 share a brightness range below 18.38 mag/arcsec². An SQM reading in that range is reported as Bortle 8–9; it cannot distinguish the two visual classes. The manual selector retains separate choices for 8 and 9.

Hover a subframe bar to see its estimated brightness and category. The integrated master is a comparison image and receives no brightness estimate. The SQM line and Bortle shading do not change Score, REVIEW or rejection decisions.

Use linear, calibrated WBPP registered frames. Luminance, green and V filters support direct calibration; RGB uses the green channel. Separate red and blue groups use green as described above. A shared calibration within each group requires matching exposure, camera, gain, binning, filter, pedestal and image dimensions. The green-to-colour transfer requires the same camera, gain, binning, image dimensions and target metadata. Other filters remain uncalibrated. Without SQM, the direct reference also needs sky-coordinate information and a local Gaia DR3/SP, DR3 or EDR3 catalog configured in PixInsight. Unreliable coordinates are rebuilt on a temporary image if necessary; source files stay unchanged.

The SQM reading should represent the sky in the target's direction near the matched exposure. An SQM-anchored plot is a relative estimate, not an independent check of the meter reading. Stars, nebulosity, gradients and changing transparency can affect the measured image background. Gaia gives a V-equivalent estimate, which differs from an SQM meter's passband. Bortle ranges are approximate.

Process icons save the reading, its local date/time and the manual Bortle choice. They do not save measurements or calibration; closing the script discards the measured session.

See the \xref{https://www.handprint.com/ASTRO/bortle.html}{approximate Bortle comparison table} and \xref{https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/pdf/2021/05/aa39587-20.pdf}{Gaia photometric transformations}.
}

\section{FWHM grading}{
Use FWHM grading on the Quality indicators tab to set personalized Good, Medium, and Bad limits. Enter the telescope aperture in Input files and observing conditions; the FWHM dialog shows this value for reference. The script uses its validated image pixel scale to convert the calculated angular limits to pixels, matching the FWHM measurements. Bands are unavailable until both aperture and pixel scale are available. The X and Y pixel scales must agree within 0.1\%. Clear Show FWHM quality bands to hide the shading. The dialog uses a single column of aligned controls: Telescope and image summarizes the current setup, Observing conditions holds altitude, wavelength and optional optical blur, and the next section sets the Good and Medium boundaries. The result shows three distinct Good, Medium and Bad rows in pixels. The information icon opens the model details, organized into labeled tables for current grading limits, reference conditions, blur contributions and filter defaults.

\table[header]{
   {{Parameter} {Initial value} {Meaning}}
   {{Aperture} {Unset} {Telescope aperture diameter in millimeters, entered in Input files and observing conditions.}}
   {{Zenith seeing} {0 arcsec} {Optional estimate entered beside Aperture; zero selects fixed seeing limits.}}
   {{Good up to, relative mode} {115\%} {Upper Good boundary as a percentage of expected FWHM.}}
   {{Medium up to, relative mode} {135\%} {Upper Medium boundary as a percentage of expected FWHM; must exceed the Good percentage.}}
   {{Good up to, fixed mode} {1.5 arcsec} {Upper seeing limit for Good quality, specified at the zenith and 550 nm.}}
   {{Medium up to, fixed mode} {2.5 arcsec} {Upper seeing limit for Medium quality at the same reference conditions; must exceed the Good limit.}}
   {{Target altitude} {45 degrees} {Fixed target altitude used for the grading model; allowed range 15–90 degrees.}}
   {{Wavelength} {From filter} {Editable wavelength for the selected filter; unknown filters start at 550 nm.}}
   {{Additional optical blur} {0 arcsec} {Optional additional blur from residual focus errors or optical aberrations; zero adds none.}}
}

With a positive Zenith seeing estimate, the FWHM grading dialog shows adjustable percentage limits. The initial Good and Medium limits are 115\% and 135\% of expected FWHM. These are chosen acceptance margins, not physical standards. Good means close to the expected performance under the supplied conditions; it does not mean equally sharp images under different seeing conditions.

With Zenith seeing set to zero, the dialog shows the two fixed seeing limits instead. These are adjustable acceptance boundaries, not measurements of the current atmosphere. The settings for both modes are retained when switching between them. Choose a target altitude appropriate for the session. A larger optical allowance permits broader stars. Guide-camera data are excluded from the FWHM grading model.

Effective wavelength starts from the selected filter. You can edit it to suit your filter; the chosen value is remembered for that filter and saved with the process settings. Switching to another filter uses that filter's saved value or its default. The Use filter default icon restores the value derived from the filter name. Names are matched without regard to capitalization:

\table[header]{
   {{Filter names} {Wavelength}}
   {{L, Luminance, G, Green} {550 nm}}
   {{R, Red} {650 nm}}
   {{B, Blue} {450 nm}}
   {{H, Ha, Halpha, H-alpha} {656.3 nm}}
   {{O, OIII, O3} {500.7 nm}}
   {{S, SII, S2} {672.4 nm}}
   {{Unknown or unavailable filter} {550 nm}}
}

Broadband values represent typical wavelengths; the exact effective wavelength also depends on the filter, camera, and source spectrum. The SII value represents the doublet near 671.7 and 673.0 nm. See \xref{https://www.astronomik.com/en/Narrowband-Filters/SII/}{SII wavelengths}.

Additional optical blur represents additional blur from the imaging optics beyond the diffraction and seeing terms. Leave it at zero unless you have an independent estimate. ASIAIR autofocus star sizes are HFD values in pixels and include several sources of blur; they cannot reliably determine this extra optical term. Star sizes from a guide camera also do not describe the imaging system's optical blur. See \xref{https://bbs.zwoastro.com/d/15167-what-units-does-asiair-use-for-star-size-in-autofocus}{ZWO's explanation of autofocus star-size units}.

The expected blur is calculated from the telescope, atmosphere, and optional optical allowance:

Total FWHM = square root of (Diffraction² + Corrected seeing² + Additional optical blur²).

Diffraction = 1.028 × 206264.806 × wavelength in nm × 0.000001 / aperture in mm.

Corrected seeing = zenith seeing × sin(target altitude)^(−0.6) × (wavelength / 550)^(−0.2).

In relative mode, use the supplied Zenith seeing to calculate expected FWHM. Good limit = expected FWHM × Good percentage / 100; Medium limit = expected FWHM × Medium percentage / 100. In fixed mode, calculate one total FWHM from each of the Good and Medium seeing limits.

The model calculates angular limits internally. Each displayed limit is converted to pixels by dividing by the script's image scale in arcseconds per pixel. The settings preview, plot labels, and measured FWHM use the same pixel units.

\table[header]{
   {{Category} {Measured FWHM}}
   {{Good, green} {At or below the calculated Good limit.}}
   {{Medium, amber} {Above the Good limit and at or below the Medium limit.}}
   {{Bad, red} {Above the Medium limit.}}
}

The bands apply to Field FWHM, Center FWHM, and the four corner FWHM plots in either plot order. FWHM measurements, quality limits and axis ticks use one decimal place. The chart header shows the indicator name and category limits. Click the information symbol button on the Quality indicators tab for the expected FWHM and observing-condition details. The button appears only when information is available for the selected indicator. Measurements and category comparisons retain their full precision; displayed rounding does not change scores. Changing the parameters refreshes the plots without repeating measurements, and the parameters are saved with process-instance settings. These bands are visual inspection guides: they do not change Score, REVIEW flags, or rejection decisions.

The root-sum-square model is exact for independent Gaussian blur components. Actual Airy and Moffat star profiles, optical aberrations, image resampling, and undersampling can depart from this approximation. Check the limits against representative images from your setup and adjust the acceptance limits as needed.

Background: \xref{https://www.eso.org/observing/etc/doc/helperis.html}{ESO image-quality model} and \xref{https://www.gtc.iac.es/observing/toolbox.php}{GTC seeing corrections}. This script uses 550 nm as the reference wavelength for its zenith-seeing inputs.
}

\section{Correlation plot}{
Open the Correlation plot tab and choose any two quality indicators with X axis and Y axis. The plot and Pearson correlation coefficient update immediately. Swap axes exchanges the selections without changing the correlation. The initial pair is Field FWHM on X and PSF signal weight on Y.

Regional KPIs appear in both selectors only when corresponding subframe measurements are available in the current group. Click Measure corners to measure missing regions and add these choices. If a selected KPI becomes unavailable after changing groups or clearing measurements, that axis returns to its initial selection; the other axis is retained when available.

Each point represents one subframe with both measurements available. The plot and correlation use the same subframes in the selected filter group, including rejected frames, independently of the review table's filter and sort order. The master is always excluded. Frames missing either measurement are omitted, and the count below the plot reports how many could not be plotted. Click Measure corners for regional indicators; load matching guiding logs for guiding indicators. Guiding values use arcseconds, FWHM uses pixels, and Below-mean Quality uses percentages.

Hover over a point to see its filename and both values. Click it to select the image in Review and measurements. If necessary, the REVIEW-only filter is cleared to reveal that image. Cyan identifies the selected image, purple a REVIEW candidate, blue another kept frame, and red a rejected frame. Plot selection does not change rejection decisions. Identical measurements can overlap; keyboard focus and Enter also select points.

The highlighted banner above the plot displays Correlation (Pearson) to four decimal places and its interpretation. Hover over the banner to see the full precision value. Strength and direction use the unrounded coefficient. The coefficient r describes the strength and direction of the linear relationship between the two KPI values. Positive means both increase together; negative means Y tends to decrease as X increases. Direction refers to the values, not whether image quality improves. The display gives r and its meaning using these continuous bands:

\table[header]{
   {{Absolute r value} {Strength}}
   {{1} {Perfect}}
   {{0.7 up to, but not including, 1} {Strong}}
   {{0.3 up to, but not including, 0.7} {Moderate}}
   {{Above 0 and below 0.3} {Weak}}
   {{0} {None: no linear relationship}}
}

The sign of r determines the positive or negative direction. Zero means no linear relationship; a nonlinear relationship may still exist. With fewer than two paired subframes, or no variation in either selected KPI, r is unavailable and the display explains why.

Axis choices are saved in process instances. Changing the plot uses the existing measurements and does not require another measurement run.
}

\section{Log folder, timing, and autofocus markers}{
Select Log folder in the directory section. All Autorun_log and PHD2_GuideLog files are read directly from this folder, regardless of letter case. Include continuation logs if acquisition was restarted. Files marked _CHN are ignored because they are translated copies. Subfolders and unrelated logs are not processed. Select the folder again to refresh its contents.

Autofocus starts from all matching logs are combined in chronological order. Overlapping logs produce one marker per start. An unreadable log is listed in Autofocus times; readable logs are still used. The report lists the source filenames for every start. If duplicate logs report different final positions for the same start, the position is marked as conflicting and the plot shows AF without a position.

 The script reads lines such as 2026/09/05 23:08:11 \[AutoFocus|Begin\] followed by the autofocus trigger description. It pairs each start with the final successful focus position from that run. For example, the later line 2026/09/05 23:11:19 Auto focus succeeded, the focused position is 692 produces the label AF 692 at the 23:08:11 start. Intermediate EAF positions are ignored. If no successful position is reported, the label remains AF. Repeated entries with the same start are deduplicated, and invalid start timestamps are reported as skipped.

Both log types use local time. Infer from image timestamps compares the local timestamp in each subframe filename with its UTC exposure-start metadata. It accounts for filenames that record exposure completion. A single unambiguous inferred offset fills the UTC offset field as its default. Select Custom UTC offset to edit this value. In automatic mode the field remains visible but is read-only. The filename timestamp must have the form YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS or YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS.

If no single offset is available, the field shows a dash. Its tooltip explains missing or multiple offsets. When several offsets are known, automatic mode continues to apply them to matching exposures. For a manual setting, choose Custom UTC offset and enter local time minus UTC in hours, such as +2 for UTC+02:00 or +5.5 for UTC+05:30. The current computer's timezone is never assumed. At a daylight-saving transition, ambiguous times remain unresolved rather than being assigned to an exposure by guesswork.

The console reports all extracted autofocus starts as written in the log and converted to UTC, together with each final focus position or not reported. Click Autofocus times to view and copy the same list. Changing the folder or timestamp convention updates the report and markers without remeasuring the images. Clearing the folder removes the markers and retains image measurements.

Autofocus markers appear only when image-quality plots use Acquisition time order. The guiding minimum, maximum, average, and quality plots omit autofocus markers and their legend. Only starts within the earliest-to-latest known subframe timestamps of the current group are shown. A start between two subframes is placed between their bar positions. Hover over the AF tag to read its log time, UTC time, final focus position, and trigger description. Markers use the start time even though the final position is reported later; no end time or duration is displayed.

All extracted starts remain in the report, including starts outside the current group's time range. If markers appear at unexpected positions, compare the UTC acquisition column with the converted times in Autofocus times and check the log's timezone setting.
}

\section{Guiding measurements and quality}{
The script matches PHD2 samples to each subframe's UTC exposure start and duration. Guide min, Guide max, and Guide avg report the combined RA/Dec displacement in arcseconds. The guider's recorded pixel scale converts its measurements to arcseconds; the imaging camera's pixel scale sets the quality thresholds.

Let p be the image pixel scale in arcseconds per pixel. The guiding quality value is:

\verbatim{
G = (minimum + 2 * average + maximum) / 4
}

The average contributes 50 percent, and the minimum and maximum each contribute 25 percent. This balances typical performance with the observed extremes. For unequal X/Y image scales, the smaller available scale is used. The UI displays the Good, Medium, and Bad boundaries in arcseconds for your setup. For example, an image scale of approximately 0.507 arcseconds per pixel gives a Good limit of approximately 0.507 arcseconds and a Medium limit of approximately 0.887 arcseconds; larger errors are Bad.

\table[header]{
   {{Category} {Guiding quality value}}
   {{Good guiding} {G is equal to or below p.}}
   {{Medium guiding} {G is above p and equal to or below 1.75 times p.}}
   {{Bad guiding} {G is above 1.75 times p.}}
}

Guiding adds a penalty of (G / p minus 1) divided by 0.75, bounded between zero and 10. Good guiding therefore has zero penalty; the upper medium boundary has a penalty of one. Weight in the Guiding group sets its relative Score weight, with a default of 25. Set it to zero to exclude guiding from Score while retaining the measurements. Guiding statistics, combined guiding quality G and their displayed thresholds are rounded to one decimal place in arcseconds. The review table, plots and guiding tooltips use the same precision. Calculations, sorting and category comparisons use the full-precision values.

Choose how Guiding in Score handles the measurements:

\table[header]{
   {{Mode} {Effect}}
   {{Require guiding data} {Use valid guiding measurements in Score. Missing or incomplete guiding data flags the subframe for review.}}
   {{Use available guiding data} {Default. Use valid guiding measurements. For a subframe without a usable guiding KPI, omit guiding from its Score and Below-mean Quality calculation. Missing guiding alone does not flag it for review.}}
   {{Ignore guiding KPI} {Exclude guiding from every Score, Below-mean Quality calculation, and review flag. The measurements remain visible for inspection.}}
}

Changing this mode recalculates the rankings immediately, preserves the measurements and manual rejection choices, and is saved in process instances. Image-quality issues can still flag frames for review in every mode.

Only valid guide measurements are used. Lost-star and rejected samples do not count as zero error. Settling samples inside an actual exposure remain part of its measurements. Guiding needs at least three valid samples, at least 90 percent time coverage, and no long uncovered interval or conflicting duplicate samples to contribute to Score. Partial statistics are marked with an asterisk. Missing timestamps, an unknown image pixel scale, or insufficient coverage require inspection in Require guiding data mode. Rejected guide samples also flag an exposure for review when its guiding KPI is in use.

Hover over a guiding column for its category, sample count, coverage, and thresholds. The selected-row details also show the guiding category or the reason it is unavailable. If an exposure is outside the loaded log coverage, add the missing continuation log and reselect the folder. Refreshing logs or changing their offset updates guiding and rankings without repeating image measurements or changing manual rejection choices.
}

\section{Move rejected originals and rerun WBPP}{
The green checkmark opens a move-plan review. It includes only manually rejected subframes with verified original files. Review the listed filenames and destination before confirming.

The script checks the complete plan before moving files. It verifies the original associations and file fingerprints, preserves each original's relative directory structure, and refuses to overwrite destination files. The calibrated images, registered images, masters, and calibration originals are not moved.

A move journal named SQR-moves followed by a timestamp and a .json extension is saved in the rejection destination. Keep it with the moved files so that you can undo the move later.

To restore a moved original, select its row and click the Undo arrow beside the green checkmark. The Undo moved originals dialog preselects that file and shows its original path. Select additional files if needed, then click Restore selected. Restored files return to their original folders and their rejection marks are cleared in the current review.

Undo also works after rescanning or reopening the script. Scan files and specify the Rejected originals folder to enable the Undo arrow, then choose the original SQR-moves journal from the rejection folder and select the files to restore. Select available checks all eligible files; Clear selection lets you start with none. You do not need to measure the images again to restore them.

Files that have changed, are missing, or have an occupied original path cannot be restored. Existing files are never overwritten. Already restored files are identified in the list. If restoration stops partway through, reopen the same move journal to restore the remaining eligible files. Each restoration also saves an SQR-undo journal in the rejection folder.

After moving, rerun WBPP from the remaining originals. Refresh its inputs and exclude the rejection directory. Use an output directory appropriate for the new run so that you can distinguish the new integration from the reviewed run.
}

\section{Common questions}{
\table[header]{
   {{Situation} {What to check}}
   {{The master is missing} {Check for an uncropped masterLight image in the WBPP master directory, with a filter identifier matching the group. Multiple possible masters require an unambiguous group-name match.}}
   {{An original cannot be rejected or moved} {Read Original association and the row details. Check that the original-light root contains the correct exposure and that matching metadata has not been removed or changed.}}
   {{There are more REVIEW rows than the chosen percentage} {Insufficient measurements also require review. Check sparse regions, Minimum stars, and missing eccentricity or corner data.}}
   {{R2 or corner plots are unavailable} {Click Measure corners beside the magnifier. R2 requires complete regional measurements; a regional plot requires a valid value for that indicator.}}
   {{A score looks good despite missing data} {Inspect its missing-data explanation. A partial score is not a complete quality assessment; missing required data is flagged for review.}}
   {{The master bar is clipped} {This is expected when it lies outside the subframe plotting range. Read the displayed master value or hover over its bar.}}
   {{Acquisition times differ from filenames} {Check whether the filename uses local time or exposure end time. The script displays and orders the full metadata timestamp in UTC.}}
   {{No autofocus markers appear} {Guiding plots intentionally omit autofocus information. For image-quality plots, use Acquisition time order, verify the log timezone, and check that extracted starts fall within the group's known subframe time range.}}
   {{Closing and reopening loses measurements} {Review data and decisions are held only in the open dialog. A saved process instance restores settings, not measured rows.}}
}
}

\make



